  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper low will continue to drift east southeast, from \ncentral Quebec today, to far northern Maine tonight/Friday, and\neventually over to Nova Scotia by Saturday. \n\nThis pattern will lead to plenty of cloud cover/mist/drizzle for\nthe rest of this afternoon and into tonight. We could see a few\nbreaks in the clouds over New Hampshire later this afternoon\nwhich could lead to some isolated/scattered showers and a \nrumble of thunder or two. RAP soundings suggest we could see 500\nto 1000 J/kg of MUCAPE over southern New Hampshire with 30 to \n35 knots of effective shear. Thus, if a storm can become \nestablished here, it could become strong with some gusty winds \nand/or small hail. Still this activity will only be hit or miss \n(and likely more miss than hit) with the best chances along and \nnorth of the mountains. \n\nWe will likely see widespread patchy fog develop over much of \nthe forecast area tonight, especially in the valleys and where \nsome of the heavier rain fell. More isolated showers are then\npossible Friday afternoon over the mountains as a few strands \nof vorticity wrap around the upper low to the northeast. Highs\non Friday should be a little bit warmer than today with fewer\nclouds outside of the mountains. Values will mainly range from\nthe lower 70s north, to the lower 80s south. \n\nThe chance for showers continues Saturday as an embedded wave \nwithin the broad cyclonic flow aloft pivots through northern New\nEngland. Associated moisture ahead of a vort max with daytime \nheating should be enough to set off widespread showers in the \nafternoon to evening. The more mild temperatures should keep \ninstability and the warm cloud depth marginal, therefore think \nwarm rain processes won't be favorable for heavy rain rates. \nThat said, a passing shower could provide a good downpour as \nwell as the possibility these congeal into broader periods of \nrain across the interior.\n\nResulting NW flow brings a drier and breezy airmass Sunday\nbefore the low completely departs. This mostly dry weather looks\nto remain in place through Wednesday, with just a few showers\npossible in the mountains daily. Temperatures warm a couple\ndegrees with highs in the upper 70s to around 80 through early\nweek.",
